   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the insurance law, in relation to insurance coverage for\nenteral formula",
    "basePrintNo": "S8924",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8924-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§3216, 3221 & 4303, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8924\n\nSPONSOR: SEWARD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the insurance law, in relation to\ninsurance coverage for enteral formula\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo amend existing insurance law to clarify the original intent of\nHannah's Law to ensure coverage of medically necessary enteral formula.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSections 1-3: Respectively, amend the following sections of the insur-\nance law: Paragraph 21 of subsection (i) of section 3216, paragraph 11\nof subsection (k) of section 3221, and subsection (y) of section 4303.\nEach section is amended to clarify that any insurance policy which\ncovers prescription drugs shall also cover oral and feeding-tube admin-\nistered enteral formulas, which are deemed medically necessary by a\nlicensed healthcare practitioner per written medical order.\n\nSubdivision 4 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThese amendments include referencing diseases and disorders originally\nintended to be encompassed within the scope of Hannah's Law; however,\ninsurance companies have been denying claims to treat conditions outside\nof those specifically cited in the statute, despite clear language that\nstates the list is not intended to be inclusive of all appropriate\nconditions.\n\nInherited enzymatic errors of metabolism and severe food protein aller-\ngies result in life-threatening symptoms and the inability to properly\ndigest and turn food into energy. Patients affected by these disorders\nmust receive nourishment from enteral or amino acid-based formulas which\nmay be administered orally or via a feeding tube in order to maintain\nproper nutrition and health.\n\nIndividuals who suffer from severe protein allergic conditions such as\nfood protein enterocolitis syndrome, (a.k.a. \"FPIES\") and rare eosino-\nphilic disorders are predominately infants and young children.  The use\nof enteral formula is the appropriate treatment to ensure that they can\neffectively metabolize nutrients critical to growth and development.\nWithout enteral formula, infants and young children suffering from these\nconditions are subject to debilitating gastrointestinal symptoms, dras-\ntic weight loss, developmental delays, mental retardation and death.\n\nSome insurance companies are interpreting the coverage requirements for\nenteral formula in Hannah's Law to apply only in instances where a\npatient's health is in decline and deemed \"failure to thrive\". Once the\npatient's health stabilizes through the use of enteral formula for\nnutrition some insurance companies have denied continued coverage which\nresults in a deterioration of their health conditions, loss of weight\nand reoccurrence of the severe symptoms associated with the condition.\nAs a result, the patient, most likely an infant or young child, has\ntheir health jeopardized and becomes at risk for sudden and unpredict-\nable deteriorations in health, thus compromising their wellbeing and\n\nsubjecting them to medical interventions and complications that would\nnot occur if their coverage was maintained.\n\nIt intent of this language is to ensure that medically necessary enteral\nformula coverage is not dependent upon a patient experiencing severe\nsymptoms and coverage must be maintained when it is determined by a\nlicensed healthcare practitioner to be medically necessary to the\npatient's maintenance of health.\n\nThis bill keeps with the original intent of Hannah's Law and clarifies\nboth methods of administration be covered per physician's written orders\nof medical necessity without the contingency of a \"failure to thrive\"\nstatus.\n\nThis language will provide further clarification to the range of\ndiseases and disorders originally intended to be encompassed under\nHannah's Law including but not limited to: inherited enzymatic errors of\nthe metabolism, severe food protein allergies and eosinophilic disor-\nders.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the first of January next succeeding the\ndate on which it shall have become a law and shall apply to all policies\nand contracts issued, renewed, modified, altered, or amended on or after\nsuch date.\n",
